export const academyLessonDeepDives:Record<string,string[]>={
"professional-standards":[
"Before leaving for a duty, check the roster, reporting time, vehicle allocation, route, passenger requirements and any client-specific instructions. Arrive with enough time to prepare without rushing. If you are unwell, fatigued or otherwise not fit to drive, report this before taking control of the vehicle. Fitness for duty is a safety decision, not something to conceal because a job is busy.",
"Passengers judge professionalism through small behaviours: a courteous greeting, clear information, patience, tidy presentation and calm responses. Keep the office informed of operational changes when safe to do so. Do not use a handheld phone while driving, argue with passengers or make promises about refunds, timings or arrangements that are outside your authority.",
"Only access or share passenger information when it is needed for your role. Keep passenger lists, contact details and safeguarding information secure, including on paper and on personal devices. A useful handover states what happened, when, who was informed, what action was taken and what remains unresolved. Avoid opinion, gossip and unnecessary personal details."
],
"vehicle-safety":[
"A walkaround is a deliberate inspection, not a memory test. Use a consistent route around the vehicle so that no area is missed. Look for fresh damage, loose or missing components, leaks, unusual smells, warning indicators and anything that has changed since the last use. Check the cab and passenger saloon as well as the exterior, because defects can affect passengers even when the vehicle appears normal from outside.",
"Pay particular attention to tyres and wheels, steering, brakes, lights, mirrors, glass, wipers, doors, emergency exits, steps, handrails, seats, seatbelts, luggage compartments and accessibility equipment fitted to that vehicle. Check the condition and security of safety equipment. A check should be appropriate to the vehicle type and the current DVSA requirements, not copied blindly from a previous vehicle.",
"Do not decide that a defect is acceptable simply because the coach is needed. Describe the location and nature of the defect accurately, report it through Freeway and follow the operator's defect process. If a defect makes the vehicle unsafe or a critical warning is present, do not use it. The vehicle must be assessed, repaired where necessary and formally cleared before returning to service."
],
"freeway-defects":[
"Freeway is Swans Travel's walkaround and defect-reporting app. The physical check comes first, the app is where the result and any defects are recorded. Select the correct vehicle, complete the check honestly and make sure that the record reflects the actual condition. Never mark an item as satisfactory simply to get through the check or because another driver used the vehicle earlier.",
"A critical defect or not-roadworthy warning is a stop instruction, not a prompt to decide whether the journey can be attempted. Do not acknowledge and proceed, clear a warning yourself or move passengers onto the vehicle. Keep it out of service, notify the office and contact Peter in Engineering. Give a clear description of what was found and where it is located.",
"Wait for Engineering to assess the vehicle and provide formal clearance before use. If the defect is repaired, ensure the record and clearance process are completed. If the warning appears inconsistent, it still needs to be resolved by the appropriate engineering process. A driver must not substitute personal judgement or timetable pressure for an authorised roadworthiness decision."
],
"drivers-hours":[
"Start by identifying the operation and the rule set that applies. Passenger transport can involve different domestic and assimilated/EU rules, exemptions and conditions. Do not assume that a rule used on one type of work applies to another. If the operation or exemption is unclear, ask the transport office before relying on a calculation.",
"Driving limits, break requirements and daily or weekly rest must be planned together. A driver should understand what counts as driving, what counts as other work and when a qualifying break must be taken. A passenger delay, late finish or pressure to complete a journey does not change the legal requirement. Raise a developing problem early enough for the office to make a safe plan.",
"Use the driver card and tachograph correctly, select the activity that reflects what you are actually doing and make required manual entries for periods not automatically recorded. Never share a card, manipulate records or ask another person to make an inaccurate entry. If a card or tachograph fails, follow the required printout, record and notification process and keep the evidence."
],
"wtd":[
"Working time is not the same thing as driving time. Vehicle checks, cleaning, loading, passenger assistance and other duties may count as work. Periods of availability have specific conditions and should not be labelled as availability just because a driver is waiting. Record what actually happened and follow the company's recording arrangements.",
"Working-time rules and drivers' hours rules operate alongside each other. A duty can appear acceptable under one set of rules and still create a problem under the other. Swans' scheduling rule is that a split duty is only used where the gap is greater than three hours. This company rule does not replace the legal definitions or remove the need to check the actual duty.",
"Fatigue is a safety risk even where a record appears compliant. Tell the office if the planned duty, an unexpected delay or personal circumstances mean you may not be fit to drive. Check records for missing or incorrect activities and raise errors promptly. Never alter a record to make a duty appear compliant."
],
"customer-service":[
"The customer experience starts before the passenger boards. A clean, presentable coach, a helpful greeting, clear destination information and a calm manner help passengers feel confident. Use language that is easy to understand and adapt your communication to the passenger. Do not assume every passenger knows the route, the stopping arrangements or what will happen next.",
"When a passenger complains, let them explain the issue without interruption where safe. Acknowledge the impact, establish the facts and explain what you can do. If you cannot resolve it, tell them how it will be passed on and notify the office. Never argue, blame a colleague or make a promise about compensation. If the coach is moving, driving safely remains your immediate priority.",
"Comfort and reliability are part of service, but they never justify unsafe driving or unauthorised stops. Explain delays honestly when information is available, keep passengers updated and avoid speculation. Take ownership of reasonable requests, report cleanliness or equipment issues and hand over unresolved complaints with enough detail for the company to follow up."
],
"safeguarding":[
"Safeguarding is everyone's responsibility. Be alert to changes in behaviour, distress, unexplained injuries, disclosures, bullying, inappropriate contact or a passenger who appears at risk. One sign alone may not prove harm, but concerns must not be ignored. Follow the company's safeguarding policy and know who the designated safeguarding contact is before starting the work.",
"If someone discloses a concern, stay calm, listen and take them seriously. Do not promise to keep it secret, ask leading questions, investigate or confront the person they are talking about. Use the person's own words when recording what was said, note the time and context and avoid adding assumptions. Do not discuss the concern with people who do not need to know.",
"Report the concern promptly through the approved company route. If there is immediate danger or an urgent medical emergency, contact emergency services as appropriate and then notify the company. Follow passenger release and collection arrangements exactly. If an adult's authority to collect a child is uncertain, keep the child safe and contact the office rather than making an informal decision."
],
"accessibility":[
"Start by speaking directly to the passenger and asking what assistance, if any, they would like. People with the same disability may have different preferences. Do not grab a wheelchair, guide dog, walking aid or passenger without consent. Give clear verbal information, allow time for a response and maintain the passenger's dignity and independence.",
"Before using a ramp or lift, confirm that you are trained on that equipment and understand the vehicle-specific instructions. Check the equipment as part of the walkaround, ensure the boarding area is suitable and follow the correct positioning, restraint and securing procedure. Never improvise, lift a passenger manually outside your training or move the coach until the passenger and equipment are safely secured.",
"Think about the whole journey, not just boarding. Keep designated spaces and access routes clear, explain stops and changes, and allow enough time for passengers to alight safely. If equipment fails, stop and follow company instructions. Do not promise a solution that has not been authorised. Record the issue and make sure the passenger is treated respectfully throughout."
],
"defensive-driving":[
"Look well ahead and keep scanning between the road, mirrors, instruments and likely conflict points. Watch for clues such as brake lights several vehicles ahead, pedestrians near crossings, parked vehicles, cyclists, junctions and changes in road surface. A hazard is not only something already in your path, it can be something that may develop into danger.",
"Choose a speed that gives you time to stop safely within the distance you can see to be clear. Leave a generous gap, increase it in wet or poor conditions and brake progressively. A coach carries standing or seated passengers who may be affected by sudden changes in speed and direction. Smoothness is part of safety, not merely passenger comfort.",
"Give vulnerable road users extra space and time. Check mirrors and blind spots before moving off, changing lanes or turning. Never assume a cyclist or pedestrian has seen the coach. If visibility, grip or traffic conditions deteriorate, reduce speed and reassess. A safe driver adapts to conditions rather than trying to maintain the timetable at all costs."
],
"reversing":[
"Before reversing, ask whether the manoeuvre can be avoided. If it cannot, stop and assess the area. Check overhead obstructions, ground conditions, pedestrians, parked vehicles, overhangs and the space needed for the coach to swing. Get out and look where appropriate. Cameras and sensors are aids, not a replacement for observation.",
"Where a banksman is used, agree the signals, position and communication method before moving. The banksman must remain visible and in a safe position. Move at a walking pace and be ready to stop. If the banksman disappears from view, a signal is unclear or a person enters the area, stop immediately and do not continue until the situation is safe.",
"Do not force a manoeuvre because other vehicles are waiting. Stop, reassess and reposition if necessary. Use repeated observations and avoid relying on a single mirror or camera view. Report damage or a near miss promptly, even if the impact seems minor. Repeated low-speed incidents can reveal a training, layout or procedure issue that needs attention."
],
"route-planning":[
"Check the vehicle allocation and its actual dimensions before planning. Consider height, width, length, weight, turning circle, axle limits and any restrictions relevant to the route. Check the destination and pick-up points, safe stopping places, access arrangements and any client instructions. A route suitable for a car or small van may be unsuitable for a full-size coach.",
"Take low bridges, weight restrictions, narrow roads, sharp turns, temporary traffic management and restricted access seriously. Use reliable route information and verify uncertain clearances. Never guess based on another vehicle passing or rely solely on satnav. If the route cannot be verified, stop safely and contact the office for a suitable alternative.",
"Reassess the plan when weather, roadworks, congestion or a closure changes the situation. Do not make a last-second manoeuvre that puts the coach or other road users at risk. Explain the issue to passengers when appropriate and keep the office informed. Build time into the plan for safe stops, checks and legal breaks."
],
"emergencies":[
"At any incident, take a breath and prioritise life safety. Stop in a safe place if possible, secure the vehicle and assess immediate hazards such as moving traffic, fire, smoke, electricity or unstable ground. Do not put yourself or passengers in danger to retrieve belongings, inspect damage or direct traffic beyond your training.",
"Call emergency services when required and provide the location, nature of the incident, number of people involved and any immediate hazards. Follow the operator's emergency and breakdown process and keep communication clear. On motorways and high-speed roads, passenger arrangements need particular care. Follow the specific roadside safety procedure and instructions from emergency services or control.",
"Once immediate safety is addressed, notify the office and record facts accurately. Identify passengers and witnesses where appropriate, note the time and location and preserve relevant information through approved channels. Do not speculate about cause or liability. Do not attempt repairs beyond your competence or place yourself in a dangerous position."
],
"fire-evacuation":[
"Treat smoke, burning smells, visible flames and relevant warning indicators seriously. Stop in a safe location if possible, secure the vehicle, raise the alarm and follow the emergency procedure. Consider the direction of smoke, traffic, wind and other hazards when choosing where passengers should move. Give short, clear instructions and check that people move away from danger.",
"Evacuation is a risk decision. Use the safest available exit and move passengers to a safe assembly point away from the vehicle and traffic. Do not allow anyone to return for luggage. Account for passengers as far as practicable and communicate any missing or injured person to emergency services. Take particular care with passengers who need assistance, without delaying urgent protective action.",
"Know where extinguishers and emergency equipment are located and understand their limitations. Only attempt to tackle a small fire if trained, it is safe, and you have a clear escape route. Never open an engine housing wide to fight an engine fire because this can feed the fire. If in doubt, evacuate and wait for emergency services."
],
"school-transport":[
"Before the journey, understand the route, collection points, passenger list, contract instructions and any individual passenger requirements. Keep lists and personal information secure. If details are missing or do not match the instructions, contact the office before departure rather than improvising.",
"Manage boarding in a calm, consistent way. Ensure passengers are seated and seatbelts are used where fitted and required. Address behaviour without shouting, threats or physical intervention outside your training and policy. Do not allow passenger behaviour to distract you from driving. Report serious or repeated concerns through the agreed school and company channels.",
"Follow the agreed passenger release arrangements exactly. Do not release a child to an adult who is not authorised, even if the adult knows the child's name or says a parent has agreed. If there is uncertainty, keep the child safe, contact the office and follow instructions. Record concerns factually and report safeguarding issues promptly."
],
"accident-reporting":[
"Immediately after an incident, protect life and make the scene as safe as possible. Call emergency services where needed and follow the relevant road traffic and company procedures. Notify the office as soon as it is safe. Do not admit liability, argue about fault or make speculative statements to passengers, witnesses or social media.",
"Record what you directly observed: date, time, location, vehicle, road and weather conditions, sequence of events, damage, injuries, passengers and witnesses. Separate your own observations from information supplied by another person. Take photographs only where safe and permitted by company procedure. Preserve relevant dashcam, telematics and vehicle information through the approved process.",
"Cooperate with the company's follow-up and provide information honestly. Report near misses and damage even if no third party is involved. An incident review should identify facts, contributing factors and prevention actions. Training may be one response, but records must distinguish learning and coaching from any separate disciplinary process."
],
"cpc-development":[
"The academy training passport can show internal learning, practical assessment, refresher dates and development activity. It does not by itself prove that a course is approved Driver CPC periodic training. Check the driver's official status and the approved training record separately, and never convert an internal course duration into CPC hours.",
"Plan periodic training against the driver's actual qualification deadline and approved hours already completed. Spread modules sensibly and check that the selected provider and course meet the applicable requirements. Keep evidence of attendance and completion in the correct CPC record system. If a record is missing or disputed, resolve it before assuming the hours count.",
"Use appraisals, practical assessments, passenger feedback, incidents and driver interests to identify development needs. Agree realistic objectives and support. A development plan should explain what the driver will learn, how the learning will be applied and how improvement will be reviewed. Training is most useful when it is relevant to real work."
],
"practical-assessments":[
"Before assessment, define the skill being assessed and the standard expected. Confirm the vehicle or equipment is suitable, the area is safe and the driver understands the task. The assessor should be competent to assess the activity and use a consistent checklist so that decisions are fair and repeatable.",
"Observe the driver carrying out the task, not just describing it. Record what was done, whether prompts were needed and any unsafe action or omission. Stop the assessment if continuing would create risk. Evidence should identify the date, assessor, driver, vehicle or equipment and the criteria observed.",
"Give clear feedback linked to the assessment criteria. If the standard is not met, explain what needs more practice and agree a reassessment plan. Do not mark a skill competent because the driver passed an online quiz or has performed it in the past. Practical competence must be supported by current evidence."
],
"remedial-learning":[
"Targeted learning should follow a review of the facts and the risk involved. A complaint, infringement, collision or near miss may indicate a knowledge gap, a practical skill issue, unclear procedures or wider operational factors. Do not assume the driver is at fault before the matter has been properly considered.",
"Choose the course and learning activity that address the specific issue. A mirror strike may call for reversing and manoeuvring learning, while a tachograph recording error may need a focused activity on manual entries. Explain the objective and allow the driver to ask questions. The assignment should be proportionate and relevant.",
"Completion is not always proof of improvement. Where appropriate, arrange a practical observation, follow-up discussion or review of later records. Record the reason for the assignment, what was completed, the outcome and any further action. Keep training and disciplinary records distinct."
]
};

